  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow \nFriday into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\nOne more mild day today, although a little cooler than yesterday\ndue to more high clouds before changes begin to occur Thursday.\n\nLight rain is expected to overspread the area late tonight/early\nThursday as low pressure from the OH Valley passes through the\nregion. Rain or showers may linger into Fri morning across\nnortheast Maryland along an inverted sfc trof on the backside of\nexiting low pressure system. \n\nStrong cold air advection begins Thu night with temperatures\nfalling from the mid 50s to the mid 40s at lower elevations and\nfrom the upper 40s Thu morning to the upper 20s by Fri morning\nat the higher elevations. Snow showers will become increasingly\nlikely across the Alleghenies in strengthening cyclonic flow \nwith the best period of snow accumulation from late Thu night\nthrough Fri evening. Even in the mountains, snow totals have\nbeen trending lower since Monday, but an inch or two of\naccumulation still seems likely. The snow will start tapering\noff Saturday as low-level curvature becomes more anticyclonic \nand cold air advection ceases. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Colder than normal temperatures and dry\nconditions expected much of next week. \n\nLongwave troughing will remain over the east coast through early \nnext week. Low level trajectories will gradually become less \nfavorable for upslope snow showers Sunday as low level ridging \nprogresses eastward from the Great Lakes. Overnight lows could be \ncolder in some locations Sunday and Monday nights since winds will \nbe lighter. While not an absolute certainty, many areas outside of \nthe urban centers and tidal shoreline will likely see their first \nfreeze by early next week. Note frost/freeze products will cease \nafter November 14 regardless of freezing observations, since it will \nbe two weeks after the average first freeze. There may be a low \npressure system that attempts to develop along the southeast coast \nby Tuesday, but at this time, it appears the strong surface high \nwill keep the local area cool and dry.",
